content.theory:
The **DOM** (Document Object Model) is JavaScript's view of your page. Every HTML element becomes an object you can grab and change.

### Selecting elements
```js
// First matching element
document.querySelector("h1");
document.querySelector("#title");
document.querySelector(".card");

// All matches → array-like list
document.querySelectorAll(".card");

// By id (fastest)
document.getElementById("title");
```
`querySelector` takes any CSS selector — same syntax you already know from CSS.

### Changing content
```js
const title = document.querySelector("#title");
title.textContent = "New title";   // change text
title.innerHTML = "<em>styled</em>"; // change HTML
```
Prefer `textContent` when you only need text — it's safer (no accidental HTML injection).

### Changing styles
```js
title.style.color = "#4f8f57";
title.style.fontSize = "28px";  // camelCase, not kebab-case
```
Better: toggle classes instead of inline styles:
```js
title.classList.add("active");
title.classList.remove("active");
title.classList.toggle("active");
```

### Creating elements
```js
const li = document.createElement("li");
li.textContent = "React";
document.querySelector("ul").appendChild(li);
```

### The classic flow
1. Grab an element
2. Change or add to it
3. Repeat
